```apex
public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * For every new line where Discount is null, set it to the product's
     * standard discount. Lines that already carry a Discount value
     * (including 0) are left untouched.
     *
     * Discount logic itself always comes from PricingRules (owned by the
     * pricing team) - we never hardcode or duplicate it here. However,
     * PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id) issues one SOQL query per call
     * (it looks up Product2.Family internally). Calling it once per line,
     * or even once per distinct product, does not scale safely for CPQ
     * quotes with up to 200 lines and many distinct products.
     *
     * Instead, we:
     *   1. Bulk-query Product2.Family ourselves for all distinct
     *      Product2Ids on the incoming lines (a single SOQL query, no
     *      matter how many lines/products are present).
     *   2. Use PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family) - the
     *      pricing team's own public, query-free method - to resolve the
     *      discount for each distinct family. The single source of truth
     *      for discount rules stays in PricingRules; we only changed
     *      *how* we invoke it to stay bulk-safe.
     *   3. Cache results by family so repeated families cost nothing
     *      extra.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Product2 prod : [
            SELECT Id, Family
            FROM Product2
            WHERE Id IN :productIds
        ]) {
            familyByProductId.put(prod.Id, prod.Family);
        }

        Map<String, Decimal> discountByFamily = new Map<String, Decimal>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount != null || line.Product2Id == null) {
                continue;
            }

            String family = familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);

            if (!discountByFamily.containsKey(family)) {
                discountByFamily.put(
                    family,
                    PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)
                );
            }

            line.Discount = discountByFamily.get(family);
        }
    }
}
```

The previous implementation called `Messaging.sendEmail` once per opportunity, which blows the **10 invocations per transaction** governor limit once more than 10 opportunities are processed (the test passes 200, triggering `LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11`). I've restructured the service to build all messages first and send them in a small number of batched `Messaging.sendEmail` calls (at most 10 invocations total, regardless of how many opportunities are passed), using `allOrNone = false` so a failure in one message of a batch doesn't prevent the others from being sent — preserving the "a single failure doesn't block the rest" guarantee without hitting the limit.

